<article class="env-lemma" id="0FXS">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FXS">Lemma <span data-tag="0FXS">29.12.2</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme such that for every point $x \in X$ there exists an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module $\mathcal{L}$ and a global section $s \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{L})$ such that $x \in X_ s$ and $X_ s$ is affine. Then the diagonal of $X$ is an affine morphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Given invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules $\mathcal{L}$, $\mathcal{M}$ and global sections $s \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{L})$, $t \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{M})$ such that $X_ s$ and $X_ t$ are affine we have to prove $X_ s \cap X_ t$ is affine. Namely, then Lemma <a href="/tag/01S8" data-tag="01S8">29.11.3</a> applied to $\Delta : X \to X \times X$ and the fact that $\Delta ^{-1}(X_ s \times X_ t) = X_ s \cap X_ t$ shows that $\Delta $ is affine. The fact that $X_ s \cap X_ t$ is affine follows from Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/01PV" data-tag="01PV">28.27.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>